1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,582
Picture a town with two main
streets, crossing at the town centre.

2
00:00:04,982 --> 00:00:09,387
A new straight road is being built,
and it cuts across both main streets.

3
00:00:09,837 --> 00:00:11,650
Diya wants to know exactly where.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,948
Can the road's equation tell us
both crossing points, at a glance?

5
00:00:16,448 --> 00:00:18,790
Yes, if we write it the right way.

6
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:20,651
Let us meet two handy forms.

7
00:00:21,301 --> 00:00:25,257
Think of the two main streets
as the x axis and the y axis.

8
00:00:25,657 --> 00:00:28,900
A line crosses the y axis
at the point zero, c.

9
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:31,486
The number c is called the y intercept.

10
00:00:31,886 --> 00:00:34,287
It crosses the x axis at a, zero.

11
00:00:34,287 --> 00:00:36,484
The number a is the x intercept.

12
00:00:36,484 --> 00:00:38,672
An intercept can be negative too.

13
00:00:39,322 --> 00:00:43,740
Watch the line y equals one
half x, plus c, as c changes.

14
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:45,897
c starts at minus two.

15
00:00:45,897 --> 00:00:47,270
Now c counts up.

16
00:00:47,270 --> 00:00:48,196
Minus one.

17
00:00:48,196 --> 00:00:49,096
Zero.

18
00:00:48,792 --> 00:00:49,692
One.

19
00:00:49,335 --> 00:00:50,235
Two.

20
00:00:50,278 --> 00:00:52,896
The line slides up, but it never tilts.

21
00:00:52,896 --> 00:00:54,591
The slope stays one half.

22
00:00:54,591 --> 00:00:56,521
Only the crossing point moves.

23
00:00:57,121 --> 00:01:00,531
Now hold c at one, and
change the slope m instead.

24
00:01:00,931 --> 00:01:03,502
The line turns around the point zero, one.

25
00:01:03,502 --> 00:01:04,402
Steeper.

26
00:01:04,125 --> 00:01:05,025
Flatter.

27
00:01:04,749 --> 00:01:05,688
Then downhill.

28
00:01:05,688 --> 00:01:07,443
The crossing point stays put.

29
00:01:08,043 --> 00:01:13,691
So in y equals m x plus c, m sets the
tilt, and c sets where the line crosses

30
00:01:13,691 --> 00:01:14,748
the y axis.

31
00:01:15,398 --> 00:01:16,824
Why does this work?

32
00:01:16,824 --> 00:01:20,192
Use the point slope form,
at the point zero, c.

33
00:01:20,592 --> 00:01:21,492
Step one.

34
00:01:21,378 --> 00:01:24,534
The line passes through
zero, c, with slope m.

35
00:01:25,284 --> 00:01:26,233
Step two.

36
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:30,884
Point slope form: y minus c
equals m times, x minus zero.

37
00:01:31,634 --> 00:01:32,694
Step three.

38
00:01:32,694 --> 00:01:34,723
x minus zero is just x.

39
00:01:34,723 --> 00:01:36,879
So y minus c equals m x.

40
00:01:37,629 --> 00:01:38,557
Step four.

41
00:01:38,557 --> 00:01:40,210
Add c to both sides.

42
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:41,928
y equals m x plus c.

43
00:01:41,928 --> 00:01:44,338
This is the slope intercept form.

44
00:01:45,088 --> 00:01:47,647
Now suppose we know both crossings.

45
00:01:47,647 --> 00:01:52,754
The line meets the x axis at a,
zero, and the y axis at zero, b.

46
00:01:53,154 --> 00:01:54,054
Step one.

47
00:01:53,964 --> 00:01:57,749
The slope from a, zero to
zero, b is minus b over a.

48
00:01:58,499 --> 00:01:59,399
Step two.

49
00:01:59,370 --> 00:02:02,377
Slope intercept form, with c equal to b.

50
00:02:03,127 --> 00:02:04,027
Step three.

51
00:02:04,003 --> 00:02:07,797
Divide every term by b, and
bring the x term to the left.

52
00:02:08,547 --> 00:02:09,493
Step four.

53
00:02:09,493 --> 00:02:12,528
x over a, plus y over b, equals one.

54
00:02:12,528 --> 00:02:14,594
This is the intercept form.

55
00:02:15,344 --> 00:02:16,435
Side by side.

56
00:02:16,435 --> 00:02:21,062
Slope intercept form shows the slope,
and the crossing on the y axis.

57
00:02:21,462 --> 00:02:24,500
Intercept form shows
both crossings at once.

58
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:27,528
a on the x axis, and b on the y axis.

59
00:02:28,128 --> 00:02:29,028
Your turn.

60
00:02:29,004 --> 00:02:32,168
x over three, plus y
over four, equals one.

61
00:02:32,168 --> 00:02:34,405
Where does it cross the y axis?

62
00:02:34,405 --> 00:02:35,694
Pause and decide.

63
00:02:38,894 --> 00:02:40,117
At zero, four!

64
00:02:40,117 --> 00:02:43,955
In intercept form, the number
under y is the y intercept.

65
00:02:44,605 --> 00:02:45,757
Remember this.

66
00:02:45,757 --> 00:02:49,774
y equals m x plus c gives
the tilt and the y crossing.

67
00:02:49,774 --> 00:02:53,780
x over a plus y over b equals
one gives both crossings.

68
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:55,380
Careful.

69
00:02:55,108 --> 00:02:59,173
To read m and c, y must stand
alone, with nothing in front of it.

70
00:02:59,573 --> 00:03:02,282
Take two y equals four x plus six.

71
00:03:02,282 --> 00:03:04,288
Divide every term by two.

72
00:03:04,288 --> 00:03:06,416
y equals two x plus three.

73
00:03:06,416 --> 00:03:09,470
Now the slope is two, and c is three.

74
00:03:10,070 --> 00:03:10,970
Quick one.

75
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:15,003
For three y equals six x minus
nine, what is the y intercept?

76
00:03:15,003 --> 00:03:16,204
Pause and think.

77
00:03:19,404 --> 00:03:20,422
Minus three!

78
00:03:20,422 --> 00:03:21,712
Divide by three.

79
00:03:21,712 --> 00:03:23,767
y equals two x minus three.

80
00:03:23,767 --> 00:03:25,393
So c is minus three.

81
00:03:26,043 --> 00:03:27,733
Now, one worked example.

82
00:03:28,083 --> 00:03:32,844
Write three x plus two y equals
twelve in intercept form, and in slope

83
00:03:32,844 --> 00:03:34,031
intercept form.

84
00:03:34,531 --> 00:03:35,431
Step one.

85
00:03:35,394 --> 00:03:37,448
Divide every term by twelve.

86
00:03:37,448 --> 00:03:41,565
Three x over twelve, plus two
y over twelve, equals one.

87
00:03:42,315 --> 00:03:43,215
Step two.

88
00:03:43,175 --> 00:03:44,075
Simplify.

89
00:03:43,973 --> 00:03:47,164
x over four, plus y over six, equals one.

90
00:03:47,164 --> 00:03:49,106
Crossings at four and six.

91
00:03:49,856 --> 00:03:50,777
Step three.

92
00:03:50,777 --> 00:03:53,448
For slope intercept form, get y alone.

93
00:03:53,448 --> 00:03:56,129
Two y equals minus three x plus twelve.

94
00:03:56,879 --> 00:03:57,827
Step four.

95
00:03:57,827 --> 00:03:59,059
Divide by two.

96
00:03:59,059 --> 00:04:02,143
y equals minus three halves x, plus six.

97
00:04:02,143 --> 00:04:05,270
Slope minus three halves, y intercept six.

98
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:07,385
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:04:07,735 --> 00:04:10,606
x over two, plus y over three, equals two.

100
00:04:10,606 --> 00:04:14,565
Many students read the intercepts
straight off, as two and three.

101
00:04:14,965 --> 00:04:17,891
But intercept form needs
a one on the right.

102
00:04:17,891 --> 00:04:18,997
Divide by two.

103
00:04:18,997 --> 00:04:21,973
x over four, plus y over six, equals one.

104
00:04:21,973 --> 00:04:24,136
The intercepts are four and six.

105
00:04:24,786 --> 00:04:26,199
Where is this asked?

106
00:04:26,199 --> 00:04:30,791
In JEE Main, for example in January
twenty nineteen: find a line from where

107
00:04:30,791 --> 00:04:32,062
it cuts the axes.

108
00:04:32,662 --> 00:04:33,828
Exam level now.

109
00:04:33,828 --> 00:04:38,099
A line through two, three makes
equal intercepts on the two axes.

110
00:04:38,099 --> 00:04:41,618
Find the area of the triangle
it forms with the axes.

111
00:04:41,618 --> 00:04:42,834
Pause and solve.

112
00:04:48,234 --> 00:04:49,648
The answer is C.

113
00:04:49,648 --> 00:04:51,338
Twenty five over two.

114
00:04:51,838 --> 00:04:54,329
Equal intercepts means a equals b.

115
00:04:54,329 --> 00:04:57,241
So x over a plus y over a equals one.

116
00:04:57,241 --> 00:04:59,366
That is x plus y equals a.

117
00:04:59,866 --> 00:05:02,340
The point two, three is on the line.

118
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:04,965
So a is two plus three, which is five.

119
00:05:05,465 --> 00:05:08,060
So the triangle has legs five and five.

120
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:10,258
Its area is half of twenty five.

121
00:05:10,858 --> 00:05:12,398
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:12,398 --> 00:05:13,981
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:17,181 --> 00:05:19,367
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:19,767 --> 00:05:20,667
One.

125
00:05:20,247 --> 00:05:23,751
What is the y intercept of
y equals four x minus seven?

126
00:05:24,651 --> 00:05:25,551
Two.

127
00:05:25,179 --> 00:05:29,837
Where does x over five plus y over
two equals one cross the x axis?

128
00:05:30,737 --> 00:05:31,637
Three.

129
00:05:31,242 --> 00:05:34,870
Why can a line through the origin
not be written in intercept form?

130
00:05:35,970 --> 00:05:38,989
Next time: a house stands
away from a straight road.

131
00:05:38,989 --> 00:05:42,016
What is the shortest walk
from the house to the road?

132
00:05:42,016 --> 00:05:43,946
Distance of a point from a line.
